  keywords  = {Summary \& Abstract PDF},
  file      = {:papers_pdf/06_2011_Lee_A_New_Grading_System_of_Lumbar_Central_Canal_Stenosis_on_MRI.pdf:PDF},
  abstract  = {[Abstract-only record - publisher full text not held.] Introduces the Lee four-grade system for lumbar central canal stenosis on axial T2 MRI, based on the degree of separation of the cauda equina: grade 0 no stenosis; grade 1 mild (all rootlets separated); grade 2 moderate (some aggregated); grade 3 severe (none separated). Four musculoskeletal radiologists graded 81 patients. Inter-reader reliability was substantial to almost perfect (ICC 0.730-0.953) and intra-reader almost perfect (kappa 0.863-0.900); grades tracked cross-sectional area and AP diameter at nearly all levels. Grounds the severity labels used by later automated stenosis systems.},
  comment   = {[Abstract-only record - publisher full text not held.] Introduces the Lee four-grade system for lumbar central canal stenosis on axial T2 MRI, based on the degree of separation of the cauda equina: grade 0 no stenosis; grade 1 mild (all rootlets separated); grade 2 moderate (some aggregated); grade 3 severe (none separated). Four musculoskeletal radiologists graded 81 patients. Inter-reader reliability was substantial to almost perfect (ICC 0.730-0.953) and intra-reader almost perfect (kappa 0.863-0.900); grades tracked cross-sectional area and AP diameter at nearly all levels. Grounds the severity labels used by later automated stenosis systems.},
  note      = {Thesis Citation: [Chapter 2: Chapter 2 (subsection: The Three Anatomical Forms of Stenosis); Chapter 2 (subsection: Complementary Information Across Sequences and Planes); Chapter 2 (subsection: Central Canal Stenosis: Schizas and Lee); Chapter 2 (section: Chapter Summary)]. Thesis Importance: Provides critical context, methodology, baseline comparison, or clinical validation standard for Selar's PhD dissertation.},
