a and von Fischer, Nathaniel and Zohrabian, Vahe M. and Muthukumarasamy, Mary Niroshinee and Vlok, Sucari and Paruk, Nafisa and Anwar, Shayan Sirat Maheen and Cruciata, Giuseppe and Islam, Omar and Siakallis, Loizos and Ikuta, Ichiro and Suri, Abhinav and Wentland, Andrew and Trivedi, Hari and Sho, Jason},
  title     = {The {RSNA} Lumbar Degenerative Imaging Spine Classification                     ({LumbarDISC}) Dataset},
  year      = {2026},
  journal   = {Radiology: Artificial Intelligence},
  volume    = {8},
  number    = {2},
  doi       = {10.1148/ryai.250480},
  url       = {https://doi.org/10.1148/ryai.250480},
  keywords  = {Full Paper PDF},
  file      = {:papers_pdf/70_2026_Richards_The_RSNA_Lumbar_Degenerative_Imaging_Spine_Classification_Lu.pdf:PDF},
  abstract  = {The authoritative peer-reviewed description of LumbarDISC, the dataset behind the RSNA 2024 challenge and the thesis: MRI studies from 2,697 patients, 8,593 series, 8 institutions across 6 countries and 5 continents, built to be the largest and most diverse expertly annotated lumbar spondylosis dataset. Inclusion required sagittal T2-like (spin echo T2, STIR or Dixon), sagittal T1 and axial T2 series covering at least 3 disc levels, patients 18+, imaged as outpatients for degenerative disease; hardware, non-degenerative pathology, severe scoliosis and heavy artifact were excluded. The label distributions are the crucial detail for anyone training on it, and they are severely imbalanced: of 13,474 spinal canal grades, 85.4\% normal/mild, 8.8\% moderate, 5.9\% severe; of 26,919 neural foraminal grades, approx. 78\% normal/mild, approx. 18\% moderate, approx. 4.5\% severe; of 26,285 subarticular grades, approx. 69\% normal/mild, approx. 19\% moderate, approx. 11\% severe. The paper also documents the annotation and curation workflow and the training/public/private test partitions, and notes that published radiologist inter-rater agreement on these grades ranges from poor to substantial depending on location - which bounds what any model trained on these labels can achieve.},
  comment   = {The authoritative peer-reviewed description of LumbarDISC, the dataset behind the RSNA 2024 challenge and the thesis: MRI studies from 2,697 patients, 8,593 series, 8 institutions across 6 countries and 5 continents, built to be the largest and most diverse expertly annotated lumbar spondylosis dataset. Inclusion required sagittal T2-like (spin echo T2, STIR or Dixon), sagittal T1 and axial T2 series covering at least 3 disc levels, patients 18+, imaged as outpatients for degenerative disease; hardware, non-degenerative pathology, severe scoliosis and heavy artifact were excluded. The label distributions are the crucial detail for anyone training on it, and they are severely imbalanced: of 13,474 spinal canal grades, 85.4\% normal/mild, 8.8\% moderate, 5.9\% severe; of 26,919 neural foraminal grades, approx. 78\% normal/mild, approx. 18\% moderate, approx. 4.5\% severe; of 26,285 subarticular grades, approx. 69\% normal/mild, approx. 19\% moderate, approx. 11\% severe. The paper also documents the annotation and curation workflow and the training/public/private test partitions, and notes that published radiologist inter-rater agreement on these grades ranges from poor to substantial depending on location - which bounds what any model trained on these labels can achieve.},
  note      = {Thesis Citation: [Chapter 2: Chapter 2 (subsection: Epidemiology and Socioeconomic Burden); Chapter 2 (subsection: The Three Anatomical Forms of Stenosis); Chapter 2 (subsection: Complementary Information Across Sequences and Planes); Chapter 2 (subsection: Sources of Acquisition Variability); Chapter 2 (subsection: Label Noise as a Performance Ceiling); Chapter 2 (subsection: From Binary Detection to Multi-Level, Multi-Pathology Grading); Chapter 2 (subsection: Chronological Synthesis); Chapter 2 (subsection: Multi-Sequence versus Single-Sequence Input); Chapter 2 (subsection: Robustness to Missing or Incomplete Sequences); Chapter 2 (subsection: Severity Distributions in Lumbar MRI Datasets); Chapter 2 (subsection: The RSNA 2024 Challenge and LumbarDISC); Chapter 2 (subsection: Label Structure, Distribution, and the Weighted Metric); Chapter 2 (subsection: What the Literature Has Established); Chapter 2 (subsection: What Remains Contested); Chapter 2 (subsection: Gap 1: The Spine Is Modelled as Independent Predictions); Chapter 2 (subsection: Gap 3: Sequence Contribution Is Assumed, Not Learned) | Chapter 3: Chapter 3 (subsection: Study Type); Chapter 3 (subsection: Unit of Analysis); Chapter 3 (subsection: Public Development Cohort: LumbarDISC)]. Thesis Importance: Provides critical context, methodology, baseline comparison, or clinical validation standard for Selar's PhD dissertation.},
}

@article{batra2025mscan,
  author    = {Arnesh Batra and Arush Gumber and Anushk Kumar},
  title     = {M-SCAN: A Multistage Framework for Lumbar Spinal Canal Stenosis Grading Using Multi-View Cross Attention},
  year      = {2025},
  journal   = {arXiv preprint},
  url       = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01634},
  eprint    = {2503.01634},
  eprinttype = {arxiv},
  keywords  = {Full Paper PDF},
  file      = {:papers_pdf/71_2025_Batra_M-SCAN_A_Multistage_Framework_for_Lumbar_Spinal_Canal_Stenos.pdf:PDF},
  abstract  = {M-SCAN: a multistage framework for grading spinal canal stenosis on the RSNA 2024 dataset (1,975 unique studies, five disc levels, Normal/Mild - Moderate - Severe), explicitly designed to fuse sagittal and axial views rather than pool slices generically. Stage one trains a U-Net to identify stenosis points on sagittal images; later stages use a multi-view, sequence-based 2.5D architecture with cross-attention to optimise feature extraction and cross-view alignment. A deliberate design choice is robustness to real-world messiness: unlike prior work assuming uniform histograms and a fixed slice count per study, M-SCAN handles a variable number of axial and sagittal slices and processes the whole spine at once for a holistic per-level prediction. Reports AUROC 0.971, state-of-the-art on this dataset; code is public. The most direct methodological comparator on the thesis's own data.},
  comment   = {M-SCAN: a multistage framework for grading spinal canal stenosis on the RSNA 2024 dataset (1,975 unique studies, five disc levels, Normal/Mild - Moderate - Severe), explicitly designed to fuse sagittal and axial views rather than pool slices generically. Stage one trains a U-Net to identify stenosis points on sagittal images; later stages use a multi-view, sequence-based 2.5D architecture with cross-attention to optimise feature extraction and cross-view alignment. A deliberate design choice is robustness to real-world messiness: unlike prior work assuming uniform histograms and a fixed slice count per study, M-SCAN handles a variable number of axial and sagittal slices and processes the whole spine at once for a holistic per-level prediction. Reports AUROC 0.971, state-of-the-art on this dataset; code is public. The most direct methodological comparator on the thesis's own data.},
  note      = {Thesis Citation: [Chapter 2: Chapter 2 (subsection: Search Strategy, Sources, and Inclusion Criteria); Chapter 2 (subsection: Sources of Acquisition Variability); Chapter 2 (subsection: Chronological Synthesis); Chapter 2 (subsection: Convolutional Networks); Chapter 2 (subsection: 2D, 2.5D, and 3D Input Representations); Chapter 2 (subsection: Cross-Sequence and Cross-Plane Fusion); Chapter 2 (subsection: Geometric Correspondence Between Planes); Chapter 2 (subsection: Multi-Sequence versus Single-Sequence Input); Chapter 2 (subsection: Challenge Solutions and the Practices They Standardised); Chapter 2 (subsection: Metrics and Their Appropriateness); Chapter 2 (subsection: What the Literature Has Established); Chapter 2 (subsection: What Remains Contested); Chapter 2 (subsection: Gap 2: Cross-Sequence Correspondence Is Available but Unused as a Training Signal); Chapter 2 (subsection: Gap 3: Sequence Contribution Is Assumed, Not Learned); Chapter 2 (section: Chapter Summary) | Chapter 3: Chapter 3 (subsection: Design Principles); Chapter 3 (subsection: Patient-Space Geometry); Chapter 3 (subsection: Competitive Methodological Baselines)]. Thesis Importance: Provides critical context, methodology, baseline comparison, or clinical validation standard for Selar's PhD dissertation.},
}

